list_one.evidence:
Lines 83-95:

    final long contentLength = unsignedExtend(in.readInt());   // line 86 — four bytes, the peer's choice
    if(contentLength < Integer.MAX_VALUE) {                     // line 87 — a cast guard, not a limit
        final byte [] buffer = new byte[(int)contentLength];    // line 88 — up to ~2 GiB committed here
        in.readFully(buffer);                                   // line 89 — ... and only now does it fail
    } else {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException(...);            // lines 92-93
    }

The only test applied to contentLength is `< Integer.MAX_VALUE` at line 87. That test exists to stop the cast at line 88 from wrapping negative; it lets every value up to 2147483646 through. Nothing compares the length against a maximum the peer does not set, against the negotiated frame size, or against the bytes actually remaining. The allocation at line 88 precedes the read at line 89, so five bytes on the wire (a type octet plus the four length bytes) commit the array. Reachable through two public entry points and two field-value cases: readLongstr() at lines 108-112 -> readLongstr(in) at 100-104 -> readBytes; readFieldValue case 'S' at line 163 and case 'x' at line 211. `unsignedExtend` (lines 45-49) deliberately clears the sign bit, so the full unsigned 32-bit range reaches the guard.

list_two.evidence:
Lines 266-270:
    private static Date readTimestamp(DataInputStream in) throws IOException
    {
        return new Date(in.readLong()*1000);
    }
`in.readLong()` returns the peer's full signed 64-bit value with no range check, and the multiplication by 1000 is Java long arithmetic, which wraps silently rather than throwing. Any value whose magnitude exceeds roughly 9.2e15 produces a product that has wrapped, so the Date handed to the application is unrelated to the seconds the peer encoded — and the peer, choosing the input, chooses the wrapped output too. Reached from readFieldValue case 'T' (lines 177-179), i.e. from inside any field table, and from the public readTimestamp() on line 274.

readBytes is declared `throws IOException` (lines 83-84) and every other malformed-input path in the file honours that contract — readFieldValue's default branch throws MalformedFrameException (lines 216-218), which is an IOException. The one exception is the else branch on lines 91-94:
    } else {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Very long byte vectors and strings not currently supported");
    }
UnsupportedOperationException is a RuntimeException. Which branch runs is decided entirely by the four bytes the peer sent on line 86: a declared length at or above Integer.MAX_VALUE selects the unchecked exception, anything below it selects the allocation path of F-001. So a remote peer, with no credential, picks the exception type that leaves this decoder.

Lines 83-95:
    final long contentLength = unsignedExtend(in.readInt());
    if(contentLength < Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
        final byte [] buffer = new byte[(int)contentLength];
        in.readFully(buffer);
        return buffer;
    } else {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Very long byte vectors and strings not currently supported");
    }
The size expression traces back four lines to `in.readInt()` on the peer's socket; `unsignedExtend` (lines 45-49) widens it to the range 0..4294967295 so the value cannot be negative. The only guard is `contentLength < Integer.MAX_VALUE`, which is a cast-safety test: it prevents the narrowing cast from wrapping and admits every value below ~2147483647. The array is committed on line 88 and the bytes are only demanded on line 89, so the allocation happens before the peer has to prove it owns the data. Reached from three places in this file: readLongstr (lines 100-104, the public readLongstr on 108) and readFieldValue cases 'S' (line 163) and 'x' (line 211), the latter two being reachable inside any field table.
